sudo

sudo stands for "superuser do". It is a Linux command that allows a permitted user to run commands with the security privileges of root (administrator) user.

Why use sudo?

Many system administration tasks require root privileges, such as:

Installing software
Updating the system
Managing users
Editing system configuration files
Starting or stopping system services

Instead of logging in as root, you can use sudo to execute a single command with elevated privileges.

sudo command
sudo dnf install httpd


to switch to root shell(have elevated permissions to execute commandsin that shell)
sudo -i
sudo su -

the sudoers file is the configuration file that controls which users or groups are allowed to run commands with elevated privileges (typically as the root user) using the sudo command.

Location
/etc/sudoers


