local DNF server

A local DNF server (or local DNF repository server) is a server inside your network that hosts RPM packages and repository metadata for systems that use DNF (Dandified YUM), the package manager in RHEL 8/9, CentOS Stream, Rocky Linux, and AlmaLinux. Instead of every server downloading packages from the internet, they download them from the local repository server.

Why Use a Local DNF Server?

Imagine you have 100 RHEL servers.

Without a local DNF server:

Every server downloads packages from Red Hat repositories.
Internet bandwidth is consumed repeatedly.
Updates are slower.
Systems without internet access cannot install packages.

With a local DNF server:

Packages are downloaded once from Red Hat.
All internal servers get packages from the local repository.
Faster installations and updates.
Useful in secure or air-gapped environments.


